5.Would you use a StatelessWidget or StatefulWidget for this screen? Justify your choice clearly, and explain the exact mechanism by which calling setState() causes Flutter to re-draw only the affected portion 
of the widget tree.

StatefulWidget: Suitable when the screen's appearance or content changes in response to user actions or asynchronous data.
StatelessWidget: Suitable when the UI is static and depends only on the constructor parameters or other external data.

How setState() works:
    1. Updates the state variable inside its callback.
    2. Marks the associated element as dirty.
    3. Schedules a rebuild for the next frame.
    4. Flutter calls build() again and reconciles the new widget tree with the existing element tree.
    5. Only the necessary layout and painting work is performed.


class CounterScreen extends StatefulWidget {
  const CounterScreen({super.key});

  @override
  State<CounterScreen> createState() => _CounterScreenState();
}

class _CounterScreenState extends State<CounterScreen> {
  int counter = 0;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Counter')),
      body: Column(
        children: [
          Text('Count: $counter'),
          ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () {
              setState(() {
                counter++;
              });
            },
            child: const Text('Increment'),
          ),
        ],
      ),
    );
  }
}



